-- title:
--   Supersingular residue pairing against the degeneracy correspondence
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ with $5 \le p$ and an algebraically closed field $K$ of characteristic $p$ with decidable equality, an integer $N \ne 0$ and a prime $\ell$. Write $F =$ `modularFunctionFieldC K N`, the subfield of Laurent series over $K$ generated by the $q$-expansions $j(q)$ and $j(q^N)$, and $R =$ `charLDegeneracyRoof K N ℓ`, generated by $j(q)$, $j(q^N)$, $j(q^{\ell})$ and $j(q^{N\ell})$; let $\alpha =$ `heckeAlphaC K N ℓ` be the inclusion $F \hookrightarrow R$ and $\beta =$ `heckeBetaC K N ℓ` the map induced by $q \mapsto q^{\ell}$. Assumed throughout: both fields carry canonical local residue data at every place, $R$ has principal divisors, at every place of either field the differential $d(\text{uniformizer})$ spans, and both Kähler differential modules are non-trivial. The hypotheses are: $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are integral ring maps ($h\alpha I$, $h\beta I$); the fibre residue identity holds along each of them, i.e. for every place $v$ of $F$, every $\omega \in \Omega[F/K]$ and every $f' \in R$ the sum of the residue terms of the pullback of $\omega$ paired with the constant family $f'$ over the fibre of $v$ equals the residue term of $\omega$ paired with the constant family $\mathrm{Tr}(f')$ at $v$ (here $\mathrm{Tr}$ is `traceFunAlong` for the map in question); $R$ is separable over $F$ via $\beta$; every $\eta \in \Omega[R/K]$ is of the form $c \cdot \beta^{*}\omega_1$ with $\omega_1 \in \Omega[F/K]$, $c \in R$; a finite set $SS$ of places of $F$ whose members are exactly the supersingular places of `ssPlaces p N K` (rational, affine geometric, with $j$-value in `ssJSet p K`); and for every place $w$ of $R$, its restriction along $\alpha$ lies in $SS$ if and only if its restriction along $\beta$ does. Given further $m \ge 1$, $u \in R$, $g_v, f_1 \in F$ and $\omega \in \Omega[F/K]$, put $g = \mathrm{Tr}_{\alpha}(\beta(g_v)\, u)$ and let $D_m =$ `weightDivisor K N m`. Suppose that at every place $x$ in `SSIndex p N K hp5 (2*m)` (that is, $x$ supersingular with $2 \le 2m$, $2 \mid 2m$, `placeWidth N x` dividing $m$ and $5 \le p$) one has $f_1 - g = 0$ or $\mathrm{ord}_x(f_1 - g) \ge -D_m(x) + 1$, and $\mathrm{ord}_x$ of the differential coefficient of $\omega$ is $\ge D_m(x) - 1$; and that at every $x \in SS$ with `placeWidth N x` not dividing $m$ one has $f_1 - g = 0$ or $\mathrm{ord}_x(f_1 - g) \ge -D_m(x)$, and the order of the differential coefficient of $\omega$ at $x$ is $\ge D_m(x)$. Then $\sum_{x \in SS}$ `kaehlerResidueTerm` $\omega$ at $x$ against the constant family $f_1$ equals $\sum_{z \in SS}$ `kaehlerResidueTerm` of $\mathrm{Tr}_{\beta}(u \cdot \alpha^{*}\omega)$ at $z$ against the constant family $g_v$, where the residue term at a place is the $K$-trace from the residue field of the local residue of the product of the given element with the differential coefficient.
--
--   This is the compatibility of the supersingular residue pairing with the degeneracy correspondence on the modular curve of level $N\ell$ in characteristic $\ell$, stated for arbitrary elements $u$, $g_v$, $f_1$ constrained only by principal-part bounds rather than for a specific lift and supersingular Hecke operator. It is the intermediate layer between the function-field identity for a pair of integral maps and the Hecke-operator statement [`ModularCurve.sum_kaehlerResidueTerm_liftFun_ssHeckeFun_eq`](thm.html#ModularCurve.sum_kaehlerResidueTerm_liftFun_ssHeckeFun_eq), which instantiates $u$, $g_v$ and $f_1$ explicitly.
